1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,710
Hi, Joanna, good to meet you.

2
00:00:03,710 --> 00:00:09,554
Now, before we discuss your new research project, I'd like to hear something about

3
00:00:09,554 --> 00:00:13,310
the psychology study you did last year for your Master's degree.

4
00:00:13,310 --> 00:00:17,220
So how did you choose your subjects for that?

5
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:22,350
Well, I had six subjects, all professional musicians,

6
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:23,980
and all female.

7
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:30,232
Three were violinists and there was also a cello player and a pianist

8
00:00:30,232 --> 00:00:31,760
and a flute player.

9
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:35,054
They were all very highly regarded in the music world

10
00:00:35,054 --> 00:00:38,166
and they'd done quite extensive tours in different continents,

11
00:00:38,166 --> 00:00:41,500
and quite a few had won prizes and competitions as well.

12
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:43,870
And they were quite young, weren't they?

13
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:50,870
Yes, between 25 and 29 - the mean was 27.8.

14
00:00:50,870 --> 00:00:55,229
I wasn't specifically looking for artists who'd produced recordings,

15
00:00:55,229 --> 00:00:58,865
but this is something that's just taken for granted these days,

16
00:00:58,865 --> 00:01:00,090
and they all had.

17
00:01:00,090 --> 00:01:00,613
Right.

18
00:01:00,613 --> 00:01:05,260
Now you collected your data through telephone interviews, didn't you?

19
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:05,310
Yes.

20
00:01:05,310 --> 00:01:12,305
I realised if I was going to interview leading musicians it'd only be possible over the phone

21
00:01:12,305 --> 00:01:13,870
because they're so busy.

22
00:01:13,870 --> 00:01:17,830
I recorded them using a telephone recording adaptor.

23
00:01:17,830 --> 00:01:20,250
I'd been worried about the quality,

24
00:01:20,250 --> 00:01:22,670
but it worked out all right.

25
00:01:22,670 --> 00:01:28,800
I managed at least a 30-minute interview with each subject, sometimes longer.

26
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:32,280
Did doing it on the phone make it more stressful?

27
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:33,964
I'd thought it might...

28
00:01:33,964 --> 00:01:39,859
it was all quite informal though and in fact they seemed very keen to

29
00:01:39,859 --> 00:01:40,280
talk.

30
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:45,289
And I don't think using the phone meant I got less rich data, rather

31
00:01:45,289 --> 00:01:46,720
the opposite in fact.

32
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:47,218
Interesting.

33
00:01:47,218 --> 00:01:52,700
And you were looking at how performers dress for concert performances?

34
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:53,445
That's right.

35
00:01:53,445 --> 00:01:57,915
My research investigated the way players see their role as a musician

36
00:01:57,915 --> 00:02:03,130
and how this is linked to the type of clothing they decide to wear.

37
00:02:03,130 --> 00:02:06,680
But that focus didn't emerge immediately.

38
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:11,258
When I started I was more interested in trying to investigate the impact of

39
00:02:11,258 --> 00:02:13,220
what was worn on those listening,

40
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:18,954
and also whether someone like a violinist might adopt a different style of clothing

41
00:02:18,954 --> 00:02:22,640
from, say, someone playing the flute or the trumpet.

42
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:27,310
It's interesting that the choice of dress is up to the individual, isn't it?

43
00:02:27,310 --> 00:02:31,263
Yes, you'd expect there to be rules about it in orchestras,

44
00:02:31,263 --> 00:02:32,700
but that's quite rare.

45
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:37,309
You only had women performers in your study.

46
00:02:37,309 --> 00:02:43,070
Was that because male musicians are less worried about fashion?

47
00:02:43,070 --> 00:02:47,452
I think a lot of the men are very much influenced by fashion,

48
00:02:47,452 --> 00:02:51,160
but in social terms the choices they have are more limited ...

49
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:56,160
they'd really upset audiences if they strayed away from quite narrow boundaries.

50
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:56,734
Hmm.

51
00:02:56,734 --> 00:03:00,750
Now, popular music has quite different expectations.

52
00:03:00,750 --> 00:03:05,874
Did you read Mike Frost's article about the dress of women performers in popular

53
00:03:05,874 --> 00:03:06,240
music?

54
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:06,870
No.

55
00:03:06,870 --> 00:03:12,227
He points out that a lot of female singers and musicians in popular music

56
00:03:12,227 --> 00:03:14,523
tend to dress down in performances,

57
00:03:14,523 --> 00:03:18,350
and wear less feminine clothes, like jeans instead of skirts,

58
00:03:18,350 --> 00:03:23,040
and he suggests this is because otherwise they'd just be discounted trivial.

59
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:28,816
But you could argue they're just wearing what's practical...I mean,a pop-music concert is usually

60
00:03:28,816 --> 00:03:30,260
a pretty energetic affair.

61
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:31,965
Yes, he doesn't make that point,

62
00:03:31,965 --> 00:03:33,670
but I think you're probably right.

63
00:03:33,670 --> 00:03:37,897
I was interested by the effect of the audience at a musical performance when

64
00:03:37,897 --> 00:03:40,010
it came to the choice of dress.

65
00:03:40,010 --> 00:03:43,970
The subjects I interviewed felt this was really important.

66
00:03:43,970 --> 00:03:48,550
lt's all to do with what we understand by performance as a public event.

67
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:54,833
They believed the audience had certain expectations and it was up to them as

68
00:03:54,833 --> 00:03:59,770
performers to fulfil these expectations, to show a kind of esteem ...

69
00:03:59,770 --> 00:04:03,070
...they weren't afraid of looking as if they'd made an effort to look good.

70
00:04:03,070 --> 00:04:03,411
Mmm.

71
00:04:03,411 --> 00:04:08,180
I think in the past the audience would have had those expectations of one

72
00:04:08,180 --> 00:04:08,862
another too,

73
00:04:08,862 --> 00:04:12,950
but that's not really the case now, not in the UK anyway.

74
00:04:12,950 --> 00:04:13,520
No.

75
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:18,674
And I also got interested in what sports scientists are doing too, with regard

76
00:04:18,674 --> 00:04:19,410
to clothing.

77
00:04:19,410 --> 00:04:22,782
Musicians are quite vulnerable physically, aren't they,

78
00:04:22,782 --> 00:04:28,080
because the movements they carry out are very intensive and repetitive,

79
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:33,400
so I'd imagine some features of sports clothing could safeguard the players from the

80
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:36,440
potentially dangerous effects of this sort of thing.

81
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:36,994
Yes,

82
00:04:36,994 --> 00:04:40,320
but musicians don't really consider it.

83
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:43,317
They avoid clothing that obviously restricts their movements,

84
00:04:43,317 --> 00:04:45,940
but that's as far as they go.

85
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:50,239
Anyway, coming back to your own research, do you have any idea where you're

86
00:04:50,239 --> 00:04:51,160
going from here?

87
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:57,240
I was thinking of doing a study using an audience, including...
